# Solution Guide Conventions

This repository contains Ansible Automation Platform solution guides published as markdown files and rendered via GitHub Pages with Jekyll.

## Markdown Blockquote Formatting

Blockquotes must use a short first line (under 12 words) followed by a blank quote line, then the detail text. This renders correctly with the site CSS which styles the first line as a colored header bar.

```markdown
# Bad -- long first line wraps ugly in the rendered CSS
> **Warning:** If your guide has fewer than 3 walkthrough steps or fewer than 2 code blocks, it is likely too thin and should be expanded or merged.

# Good -- short label, details below
> **Warning:** Your guide may be too thin.
>
> If it has fewer than 3 walkthrough steps or fewer than 2 code blocks, either expand the scope or merge it with a related guide.
```

Accepted label prefixes: `**Tip:**`, `**Warning:**`, `**Why [tool]?:**`, `**Example ...**`, `**RBAC:**`, or any short bold descriptor.

## External Links Open in New Tabs

All links to external sites (anything not on this GitHub Pages site) must open in a new tab. The Jekyll layout includes a script that handles this automatically for the rendered site, but when writing new HTML `<a>` tags with external URLs, always include `target="_blank"`:

```html
# Good
<a target="_blank" href="https://www.redhat.com/...">Red Hat</a>

# Bad -- missing target
<a href="https://www.redhat.com/...">Red Hat</a>
```

Internal cross-references between solution guides should use plain markdown links without `target="_blank"`:

```markdown
See [AIOps automation with Ansible](README-AIOps.md) for the full pipeline.
```

Many guide bodies wrap content in `{% raw %}…{% endraw %}`, so **Liquid does not run** inside them. For HTML `href` attributes in those files (for example use-case strips), use a plain Pages path with `.html`, not `relative_url`:

```html
# Good -- works inside {% raw %} on GitHub Pages
<a href="README-AIOps-Use-Case-01-Incident-Ticket-Enrichment.html">…</a>

# Bad -- Liquid left as literal text inside {% raw %}
<a href="{{ '/README-AIOps-Use-Case-01-Incident-Ticket-Enrichment' | relative_url }}">…</a>

# Bad -- GitHub Pages serves raw markdown for .md URLs
<a href="README-AIOps-Use-Case-01-Incident-Ticket-Enrichment.md">…</a>
```

Hub pages with YAML front matter and no `{% raw %}` wrapper (for example `aiops-use-cases.md`) may keep using `{{ '/path' | relative_url }}`.

## Solution Guide Structure

All solution guides must follow the framework in `README-best-practices.md`. The framework section names map 1:1 to the section names in every guide:

1. **Title** -- outcome-oriented topic in `<h1>` with a `guide-type-badge` pill (Solution Guide, Implementation Guide, or AIOps Use Case for the ticket enrichment appendix). Do not append the guide type to the `<h1>` text. Operational patterns (six use cases, Crawl/Walk/Run) belong in [README-AIOps.md](README-AIOps.md#common-aiops-use-cases), not separate catalog entries.
2. **Overview** -- Problem statement (2-4 sentences), hero callout, table of contents.

### Guide hero callout (below `guide-header`)

- **Foundational AIOps** ([README-AIOps.md](README-AIOps.md)): `guide-hero-callout` with AAP icon and **Ansible unlocks AIOps** text (no partner logo).
- **Partner Solution and Implementation guides**: `guide-hero-callout guide-hero-partner` with AAP icon, `+`, and partner wordmark at **36px** height, left-aligned in the same lavender box. Use light/dark logo pairs from `assets/images/logos/` when available. Add a modifier class (`guide-hero-partner--splunk`, `--netbox`, `--redhat-ai`, etc.) when the wordmark needs sizing tweaks (see `assets/css/style.scss`).
- **AIOps Use Case** pattern pages: no partner hero unless a single vendor is the focus; link to the foundational guide instead.

```html
<div class="guide-hero-callout guide-hero-partner guide-hero-partner--splunk" role="img" aria-label="Ansible Automation Platform and Splunk">
  <img src="assets/images/logos/aap-ansible-icon.png" alt="" class="guide-hero-callout__icon" width="36" height="36">
  <span class="guide-hero-partner__plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
  <span class="guide-hero-partner__partner card-partner-logo-set">
    <img src="assets/images/logos/splunk-logo.png" alt="Splunk" class="card-partner-logo card-partner-logo--light">
    <img src="assets/images/logos/splunk-logo-dark.png" alt="" class="card-partner-logo card-partner-logo--dark" aria-hidden="true">
  </span>
</div>
```
3. **Background** — Domain context explaining what the topic is and why it matters.
4. **Solution** — Components list, Who Benefits persona table, demos/videos.
5. **Prerequisites** — AAP version, collections (with Automation Hub links), external systems.
6. **[Topic] Workflow** — Architecture diagrams, narrative walkthrough.
7. **Solution Walkthrough** — Step-by-step technical depth with featured code.
8. **Validation** — Concrete test, expected output, troubleshooting table.
9. **Maturity Path, Summary, and Related Guides** — Crawl/Walk/Run, then Summary (wraps up the guide), then Related Guides (call to action for what to read next). Summary always comes before Related Guides.

Key non-negotiables:

- **Solution not tutorial**: If it could be titled "Getting started with X," reframe it around the problem it solves.
- **Starter template**: New guides should copy the skeleton from the Appendix of `README-best-practices.md`.
- **Minimum depth**: At least 3 walkthrough steps, 2 YAML code blocks, 1 diagram, ~800 words.
- **Validation section is mandatory**: Include a concrete test, verbatim expected output, and a troubleshooting table.
- **Automation Hub links**: Every Ansible collection referenced must link to its Automation Hub page.
- **KB article link (legacy only)**: If a corresponding article exists on access.redhat.com, include the link in a blockquote under the title. New guides do not require a KB article.
- **Lightspeed naming**: Follow `.cursor/rules/red-hat-lightspeed-terminology.mdc` for Automation code assistant, Automation intelligent assistant, and Red Hat Lightspeed in new prose and catalog copy.
- **Automation orchestrator naming**: Follow `.cursor/rules/red-hat-automation-orchestrator-terminology.mdc` (**Automation orchestrator**, not title case on the second word).

## Scoring and Reviewing Guides

When reviewing or grading solution guides, use the weighted rubric from `README-best-practices.md`:

| Category | Weight |
|----------|--------|
| Outcome Clarity | 20% |
| Architecture Clarity | 20% |
| Technical Executability | 25% |
| Validation/Testability | 15% |
| Production Readiness Info | 10% |
| Business Framing | 10% |

Score each category 1-5. Multiply by weight for a final score out of 10. Any category below 3 means revise before publishing.

Apply category-aware adjustments for AIOps, Network automation, and AIOps Use Case pages (see "Scoring by Solution Category" in `README-best-practices.md`). Do not penalize Network automation guides for lacking AI components.

When writing a review:
1. List **Strengths** (what the guide does well).
2. List **Weaknesses** (specific gaps, not vague complaints).
3. List **Suggestions** (actionable improvements with examples).
4. Provide an overall score and a one-line verdict.
5. If reconciling with another reviewer, document the reasoning for any score changes.

Solution guide reviews are stored in `opus-review-solution.md`. Implementation guide reviews are in `opus-review-implementation.md`. The [Reviews hub](reviews.md) links to both.

Implementation guides follow `README-best-practices-implementation.md` -- a separate framework and rubric from solution guides. The [Best Practices hub](best-practices.md) explains when to use each.

## Mermaid Diagrams

Workflow and architecture diagrams must use fenced `mermaid` code blocks, not ASCII art. The site layout loads Mermaid v11 via CDN and renders them automatically. GitHub also renders mermaid natively in markdown previews.

```markdown
```mermaid
graph LR
  A([Event]) --> B[Process] --> C([Result])
`` `
```

Use `graph LR` for workflows (left-to-right) and `graph TD` for architecture (top-down). Keep diagrams to 3-8 nodes. See `.cursor/skills/mermaid-diagrams/SKILL.md` for full conventions and examples.

## Jekyll and GitHub Pages

- Layout: `_layouts/default.html` (includes nav header, footer, Prism.js syntax highlighting, Mermaid.js diagram rendering, copy-to-clipboard on code blocks, and the Konami code Easter egg).
- Styles: `assets/css/style.scss` (extends the `jekyll-theme-dinky` base theme with Red Hat branding).
- Config: `_config.yml` controls excludes and theme settings.
- Files excluded in `_config.yml` are not rendered on GitHub Pages. If a markdown file should be publicly accessible, ensure it is not in the exclude list.

## File Naming

- Solution guides: `README-[topic].md` (e.g., `README-AIOps.md`, `README-IA.md`)
- Main entry point: `README.md` (table of contents only)
- Best practices hub: `best-practices.md`
